<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">ADS-L, <span class="highlight">the common language</span> of everything that flies</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    The European electronic conspicuity standard, published by EASA. It
    covers everything from light aircraft to skydivers, by way of
    microlights, gliders, gyroplanes, helicopters, balloons and paragliders,
    and tomorrow drones.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>Open.</h3>
      <p>A public specification that anyone can read and implement, and that skyBlip implements as open source.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Lightweight.</h3>
      <p>A frame of a few milliseconds, once a second, on the licence-free 868&nbsp;MHz band. A pocket device on a battery is enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Interoperable.</h3>
      <p>Its channels follow those of FLARM and PilotAware. skyBlip Go also receives FLARM traffic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">See and be seen</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">See the traffic</p>
      <h3>A radar <span class="highlight">read at a glance</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the centre, track up. Traffic at the upper right is off the
        right of the nose, nothing to convert. The shape tells you whether it is
        above, level or below, and a line shows where it will be in a minute.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft, one above, one below, one level and climbing, each with its one-minute line" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Know where to look</p>
      <h3>An alarm <span class="highlight">that gives the direction</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Within 3&nbsp;km and 300&nbsp;m, a quarter of the screen flashes on the
        side of the traffic, and the radar takes over whatever page is showing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during an alarm: the quarter of the screen facing the traffic has turned black" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Be seen</p>
      <h3>Your position <span class="highlight">broadcast every second</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        In flight, every second, it broadcasts your position in ADS-L. Every
        aircraft fitted with it sees you, with your callsign.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "nearby.png", alt: "The Nearby page: five aircraft nearest first, with their source ADS-L or FLARM, their callsign, their distance and their relative altitude" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Keep your habits</p>
      <h3>The traffic <span class="highlight">in your app</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Over Bluetooth, the traffic shows up in SDVFR Next, SkyDemon,
        ForeFlight and the others. Three devices at once, no pairing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo shot--app">
      <%= pages_image_tag "sdvfr-next-traffic.avif", alt: "SDVFR Next: the map with the traffic received from skyBlip Go, each aircraft labelled with its callsign" %>
      <figcaption>SkyDreamSoft SDVFR Next <a href="https://skydreamsoft.fr">skydreamsoft.fr</a></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p class="more"><%= link_to_page "features", label: "See every feature" %></p>

  <%= render "shared/cta" %>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">At every stage <span class="highlight">of the flight</span></h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>Before take-off</h3>
      <p>
        Set down in the cockpit, it looks for its position and tells you how far
        it has got. Aircraft type and callsign are set on the device.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>In flight</h3>
      <p>
        It broadcasts your position every second, plots the traffic track up,
        and warns you of traffic closing in.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>After landing</h3>
      <p>
        It keeps its sixteen most recent flights and updates from the browser. Switched
        off, the screen keeps the logo without drawing power.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

